ad from -7.2 to -13.3. The engine preferred B4. This was the first big drop, and the game never came back from it."}, {"turn": 17, "text": "F2 dives under White's F3 stones and lost 9.4 points (-12.9 → -22.3). The engine wanted G6 instead. By move 29 the F2 stone had been captured, which is White's one capture."}, {"turn": 23, "text": "D2 lost 13.7 points, taking the lead from -26.2 to -39.9. The engine's choice was G2. Black kept crawling along the second line while White's E2 and E3 stones stayed solid."}, {"turn": 29, "text": "H3 lost 14.4 points (-34.3 → -48.7), GPT-6.1 Sol's worst move of the game. The engine chose H6. After it, the lead stayed near -48 for the next several moves."}, {"turn": 36, "text": "The 20k's G1 lost 20.7 points, moving the lead from -50.7 to -30.1, its worst move by far. Black had just built a wall of H1 to H4 and J4 on the right edge. The engine's choice was B4, which White played on move 38. Even after this slip Black was still 30.1 points behind."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "The `inspect` function in command 4 only parses the current board, groups chains/liberties/regions, and computes captures for a single move the player named; `doPlay`, the SGF/replay validation in command 60, and the notes written in command 61 are logging and records. Nothing loops over empty points to score moves or plays continuations forward, so no move search is present.", "stop": false}}